diff --git a/src/requests/adapters.py.md b/src/requests/adapters.py.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..18d7cd2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/requests/adapters.py.md
@@ -0,0 +1,24 @@
+---
+title: "adapters.py"
+date: 2026-09-23
+hash: "sha256:cd2f154879200ad5d9b08429ae1c664a631fddd3a67a2895be4d74fabf18b3da"
+---
+## Decisions
+- The default timeout for a Session's requests lives on `HTTPAdapter` (`HTTPAdapter(timeout=...)`, mounted on
+  the session), not on `Session`. Product owner, 2026-09-23: keep to the maintainers' split — Session holds
+  HTTP state, the transport adapters hold connection behaviour, timeouts included (see
+  `sessions.py.md`). This rules out any Session-side route to the same effect, including a Session helper
+  that builds and mounts the adapter.
+- A send's `timeout=None` means "not given" and gets the adapter's default. It is not an opt-out: `Session`
+  forwards `timeout=None` whether or not the caller passed it, so the adapter cannot tell the two apart
+  without Session changes that the Session decision rules out. The per-call "wait forever" is
+  `timeout=(None, None)`.
+- A call's own timeout replaces the default as a whole. There is no per-component merge (a default
+  `(3, 10)` with a call's `(None, 5)` sends connect=None, read=5): a tuple's `None` is an explicit
+  "no limit" for that phase, and merging would take it away.
+- The default is validated when the adapter is constructed, with the same rules and messages as a per-call
+  timeout. A bad value then fails where it was configured, not on the first request (possibly long after).
+
+## Discussions
+- `BaseAdapter` was left without a default-timeout contract. Only `HTTPAdapter` has a caller that needs it;
+  a third-party adapter that wants a default can hold its own.
